Page layouts for the Xymon web pages.

Every page header loads layout.css and layout.js from the directory that
XYMONLAYOUT in xymonserver.cfg names, after xymonbody.css; see
xymonserver.cfg(5). A layout changes how the pages look and adds what a
script can, on top of the HTML every page already has. It does not change
what the pages show.

classic: the pages as they have always been; both files are empty.
minimal: as little as possible -- a flat dark page, a small system font,
    compact tables without frames, the menu as a plain row of words, and
    the colour of the page as a thin line at the top. No script.
modern: a dark page with the groups of hosts as cards and the menu as one
    bar across the top, its bottom edge in the colour of the page. Its
    script adds a summary bar below the top line: a count per colour, and
    of recent and acknowledged statuses, where a click shows only the rows
    with that status, and a countdown to the next refresh that can be
    paused. It finds the statuses by their icons' xmdot-<colour> classes,
    or by the icons' file names on pages that have no classes.

A layout's script may take over the page refresh: the six headers that
refresh their page keep the <meta refresh> inside <noscript> and start a
plain reload timer, window.xymonRefreshTimer, which the script can clear
and replace; window.xymonRefresh holds the interval in seconds.

index.txt lists the layouts here, for the Look and feel page, which
previews them; add the name of a layout you install.

Both files are plain ASCII: the pages may be served without a character
set, and a browser then reads them in its own default one.
